WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.490
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Welcome to
Cloud OnAir, our live webinars

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.930
from Google Cloud.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.700
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.230
and we're really happy
to have you here with us.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.360
My name is Yuri Grinshteyn.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.030
I'm a customer engineer
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.190
And I'm incredibly happy
and excited to have

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:27.392
Carter Maslan, the founder and
CEO of Camio with us today.

00:00:27.392 --> 00:00:29.100
Carter, thank you so
much for being here.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.660
CARTER MASLAN: I'm
happy to be here.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.785
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:34.254
You can ask questions
any time on the platform.

00:00:34.254 --> 00:00:36.170
We'll have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.629
We'll also be doing a live
Q&amp;A later in the program.

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:41.920
So first of all, Carter,
again thank you for being here

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.549
and congratulations on
your Best of CS award.

00:00:44.549 --> 00:00:45.590
CARTER MASLAN: Thank you.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.700
Just extremely
excited about that.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.990
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Great.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.640
So today we'll be
talking about how

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.890
you have built Camio
to provide machine

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.830
learning for video
intelligence to your customers.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.780
So perhaps we just
start by you telling us

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.420
the problem you
set out to solve.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.330
CARTER MASLAN: So Camio is
real time video search that

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.950
works with any camera stream.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.920
And the big problem
with video today

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.920
is that in recording
hours and hours of video,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.140
there's only a certain
small fraction of it

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.150
that's actually warranting
any attention from humans.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.210
And so getting machines
to do that work for you

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.540
to highlight what's
interesting is the core

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.580
of what Camio is about.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.210
So that real time video
search will automatically

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.580
identify the few
two minutes perhaps

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.956
each out of every day that
is warranting your attention.

00:01:32.956 --> 00:01:34.080
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.620
So why don't we have you
show us exactly what this

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.685
looks like in practice.

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:39.935
CARTER MASLAN: That'd be good.

00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:43.640
We'll jumped to a demo where
I can just do a quick search.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.690
And I'll just start by
looking at a single camera

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:56.560
like Liverpool, which is a
public library in New York.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.240
And the first thing to notice is
that there's no dead air time,

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.150
so the first level
of machine learning

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.520
is just highlighting
interesting events

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.670
that warrant your attention
over a large period of time.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.070
But you can do quick
searches like this.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:14.870
I can just say I'm looking
for people in red or blue

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.985
on the sidewalk, and now out
of two- or three-week period,

00:02:19.985 --> 00:02:21.860
I'm seeing every time
there was a person that

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.030
was either in red or blue
across the sidewalk including

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.660
a quick chart for the frequency.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.785
You can just see this is all
the times that people in blue,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.160
all the times people in red
are-- all the time that there

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.820
were people period.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.430
And that same thing applies--

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.730
you can still play the
video behind the event,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.740
but you can also do
advanced searches

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.010
like if I only want
to see when there were

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.240
bikes across all the cameras.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.580
You could just type
in bikes, and now this

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.030
is using CNN's to label
the events for the cases

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.120
where there was a bike.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.780
And like I said
before, you can play

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.360
even this person that's
dedicated in winter time snow

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.980
biking.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:05.480
It'll find that.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.670
You can also do a quick
search for something

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.040
as advanced as snow plow.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.750
So I'll just do a quick search.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.400
Snowplow for that same period.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.910
And so this is just
a quick example.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.790
All these demos are things that
you can open in your browser

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:26.000
and do yourself
at Camio.com/demo.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.650
There's a sequence
of links that you

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.660
can open without
any kind of auth

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:32.495
and follow along as we do demos
throughout this discussion.

00:03:32.495 --> 00:03:33.620
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:33.950
Thank you.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.990
So that looks really cool
and really impressive.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:39.860
You're able to very quickly
find interesting things

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.310
in these videos, and
the UI is really fast.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.610
And it looks like it can
provide a lot of value.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.370
Can you give us a sense of
who might actually use this?

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.360
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.350
So anybody that has cameras,
large corporate campuses,

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.440
cities, even homes are using it.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.425
But it's basically--
the core value of Camio

00:04:00.425 --> 00:04:02.600
is that it can work
with video streams

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.060
from old camera
systems, existing camera

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.856
systems, your
existing DVR, or NVR,

00:04:07.856 --> 00:04:09.230
and make it into
something that's

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:14.030
smart and searchable and
browsable so that it learns

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.489
what you find interesting in
each individual camera stream.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:18.860
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:27.070
All right so let's go back to
our content and resume there.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.330
And I think the next thing our
audience would very much like

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.970
to understand is some
of the decisions that

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.620
went into building Camio.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.040
And first you know why did
you choose to build it on GCP?

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.600
CARTER MASLAN: Well, we--

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.220
our founders came
from Google, so we

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.890
were familiar with
that at the beginning.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.350
But the core reason
that we chose GCP

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.160
was the dynamic scalability
and quick deployment time.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.040
So we deployed
several times a day.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.440
And as a startup,
your core advantage

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.840
is just listening to what
customers want and delivering

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.430
it quickly and to be able to hit
the deploy button several times

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.940
a day without the hassles of
any kind of hardware issues

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.560
or maintenance issues.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.490
That was a big one.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.370
But then the core of
the problem is just

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.330
matched to the dynamic scaling.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.710
So this chart--

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:18.690
I don't know if you can present
this chart of a real life

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:23.670
deployment with 62 cameras
that shows the number of events

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:28.920
over the time of day, and you
can see that it's very spiky.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.640
It's five times the traffic
during a daytime shopping

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.530
mall versus the nighttime.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.640
And when you have that kind of
a problem where you're saying,

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.530
well, tell me what's interesting
out of hours of video

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.230
with this kind of
spiky activity level,

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.550
having the dynamic provisioning
automatically scale

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:49.530
your compute resources to
match what you're analyzing

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.390
is a key strength.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.300
So rather than having to
deal with on-site, on-premise

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.480
hardware that has to be
provisioned at peak capacity,

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:02.820
you can instead provision at a
much lower dynamic rate that's

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.855
much more cost efficient and
much more bandwidth efficient.

00:06:05.855 --> 00:06:06.660
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, I think you

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.076
make a really
important point here

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:10.140
is that it would be really
difficult to achieve

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.080
optimization using any kind
of a traditional on-premise

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.690
infrastructure with a
workload and a demand that

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:16.560
looks like this.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.750
This is incredibly spiky.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.900
Like you said, I think you're
delta between your baseline

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.850
here and your peak is--

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.730
it looks to be at
least 5x if not more.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:26.710
CARTER MASLAN: Right.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.320
You have some
cameras that might be

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.510
like a mechanical
room or a server room

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:33.240
where credit card data
is processed where

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.844
there's very low activity.

00:06:34.844 --> 00:06:37.260
And then you've got others
that are like the main entrance

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.340
for a shopping mall.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.280
And so it's not
just hour of day,

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.250
but it's by the
venue of the camera.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.470
And so making that
cost efficient

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:51.150
is the core focus is how do
you take the mass of millions

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.010
of existing cameras
and connect them

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.980
to state of the art machine
learning at a price point that

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.022
makes sense for people on
a business perspective.

00:06:58.022 --> 00:06:58.938
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah.

00:06:58.938 --> 00:06:59.490
Absolutely.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.096
Makes a lot of sense.

00:07:01.096 --> 00:07:02.470
So this looks
really interesting.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:06.280
Let's move on to how
this actually works.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.020
So it looks like there's really
four major components to Camio.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.297
There's the customer's
on-premise infrastructure,

00:07:12.297 --> 00:07:14.130
which is what they need
to collect the video

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:16.430
and send it to the
cloud for processing.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.892
There's all this stuff
that happens in the cloud.

00:07:18.892 --> 00:07:20.850
There's the user experience,
which is delivered

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.640
via web and native mobile apps.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.440
And then there are
the hook [INAUDIBLE],,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.280
which we'll cover
a bit later on.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.490
So let's first start by talking
about what does the customer--

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.090
viewers need to really do this.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.466
We'll start with the cameras.

00:07:34.466 --> 00:07:36.090
I think you said this
already that they

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.370
can use their existing
cameras as well as new ones,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.680
but are these special
purpose cameras

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.851
or can they just be any camera?

00:07:42.851 --> 00:07:44.850
CARTER MASLAN: They can
literally be any camera.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:48.990
The nice thing is that Camio
analyzes the H.264 video

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:52.950
stream itself that comes
from the camera directly

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.760
or from a network video
recorder like a DVR

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.260
or a video management
system, a VMS.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.050
And so no matter
where it comes from,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.870
your existing cameras,
even analog cameras

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.360
that are connected
through one of those NVRs

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.700
can be accessed via Camio.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.880
So in terms of a
customer's deployment,

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.680
they can light up
their existing cameras

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.810
in less than 10
minutes because as long

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:18.879
as the cameras are on a network.

00:08:18.879 --> 00:08:19.920
That's all that's needed.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.510
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.240
So then the second part of
their on-premise infrastructure

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.550
would be the network video
recorder or video management

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:27.540
systems.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:31.770
So, one, is that a required
component, and, two,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.100
how does that system
really play into this?

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.919
CARTER MASLAN: So the
network video recorder or VMS

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:41.700
is usually used to provide the
complete archive of HD video

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.530
on site, on premise.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:47.190
So you can either use that
or go direct to the cloud.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.810
You can choose.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:52.020
So it becomes mainly
an issue of what's

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:55.280
your uplink bandwidth, what
are your archiving retention

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.280
requirements and things.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.570
But you're free to serve the
video streams from the NVR

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:01.650
or from the camera.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:02.700
It doesn't matter which.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.020
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Very cool.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.270
And then the third part of
their on-premise deployment

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.950
is this thing that's
just called box.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.510
Well, that sounds
awfully mysterious.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.343
Can you tell us a bit
about what the box is?

00:09:11.343 --> 00:09:12.400
CARTER MASLAN: Yes.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:13.950
The box is a video gateway.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.940
It's basically a device that
analyzes the video streams

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:19.180
on the local network.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:22.200
So you get a huge
bandwidth efficiency gain

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.270
because all of the analysis
and metadata that's

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.630
observed about each
12-second video event

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.820
is analyzed locally by the box.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.665
And the box is in this
kind of chatty conversation

00:09:34.665 --> 00:09:37.040
with the cloud saying, hey,
do you find this interesting?

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:38.930
Based on what you've
seen the user do,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:40.670
do you think the
user wants this?

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:43.760
And all of those decisions
in that chatty conversation

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.970
are determining what
happens subsequently

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:48.160
in analyzing the video.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.910
And that's where the
big cost efficiencies

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:54.110
come from because
you saw the spikes.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.550
If a camera is--

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:58.460
even with high activity,
if only a fraction of it

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:02.390
is interesting, like when a
person approaches the server

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:05.060
room that houses your
credit card, data that's

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:08.369
when you want HD recording with
full analysis and integration

00:10:08.369 --> 00:10:10.410
with their security
operations center and things.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.560
So all of those
kinds of decisions

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.540
are made locally by the machine
learning that's running on box.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:20.570
And we'll talk about later what
machine learning unbox does,

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:25.310
but the second thing that box
does is gives you security.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:26.874
So you remember the Mari virus?

00:10:26.874 --> 00:10:27.790
YURI GRINSHTEYN: I do.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:30.380
CARTER MASLAN: So that
was a massive attack

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.770
that was using people's cameras
and DVRs, the vulnerability

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:37.700
in them, because the traditional
deployment model for cameras

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.220
on corporate campuses
and things for when

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.730
you need remote viewing was
to forward inbound ports.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:48.830
And since box is only
over SSL port 443,

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.920
you can completely
close your local network

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.170
to inbound network requests.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:57.260
So in addition to its
efficiency function,

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:59.270
there's also a really
good security function

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:02.000
because you can now
close all inbound ports

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:05.510
on your local networks to avoid
a repeat of the Mari virus.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.760
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:08.259
All right, so we've
got our cameras.

00:11:08.259 --> 00:11:09.800
They're capturing
all this video.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.760
We have their NVR or VMS that's
archiving all the HD video.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.730
And we have the box that's
basically efficiently choosing

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.430
which bits to actually send
from the cloud for processing.

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:21.380
So which takes us
to the part that I

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:25.340
think most folks online are here
to actually see, which is what

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:26.910
are you doing in the cloud.

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:28.680
So let's move into that.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:32.030
Let's cover three major
paths that I think

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:34.010
are going to be interesting
to our audience.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:38.330
First is what you're doing with
that video when it arrives.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:41.120
The second one will be how
you were delivering that user

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:41.780
experience.

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:42.930
You showed us the UI.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:43.940
It's incredibly fast.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.850
It's very responsive, and it's
operating on obviously what

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:48.560
are fairly complex datasets.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.080
So I'd love to hear about
how you're really making

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:53.300
that as performant as it is.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.160
And then thirdly
we'll talk about how

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:58.070
you're taking both the videos
data as well as the user

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:01.420
interaction data and combining
them to power your ML.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:02.190
That's sound good?

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.080
CARTER MASLAN: That sounds good.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:04.400
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So let's
talk about the video first.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:05.570
You've got your cameras.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:06.980
You've got your box.

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.690
Box sends video to the cloud.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:09.805
What happens then?

00:12:09.805 --> 00:12:11.540
CARTER MASLAN: So
as I mentioned,

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:13.130
there's a front end
that's basically

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.060
in that chatty
conversation with the box.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.510
And so we use App
Engine Standard

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.940
as that front end for
the dynamic scaling

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:23.780
and responsiveness to both
APIs, queries, and end

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.090
user search API queries.

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:28.220
And so that is the
initial entry point

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.920
is the Google front end that's
talking to an App Engine

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.860
Standard set of instances.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.110
And then those decisions
made at the beginning

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.620
based on the trained
models to that point

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.570
determine all the
subsequent workflow

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:44.730
that you see on the
right side of this chart.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.780
So what level of
analysis should get--

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.670
should be applied to this?

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.860
Where should the storage go?

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.390
So if you want to just
have a response that says,

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:58.070
hey, for this video, send
it to Nearline Storage

00:12:58.070 --> 00:12:59.760
because it's unlikely
to be reviewed.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:03.570
So for cost efficiency, just
let me give you a sign GCS URL

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:07.880
and just send it
straight to GCS versus

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:11.540
this is an important event, so
lets call hooks that are things

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:15.560
like the Google Vision API or
other specialized classifiers

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.690
because this is
particularly important.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.640
All of that workflow
is determined

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.860
after it goes
through the front end

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:24.590
with these dynamically
provisioned App Engine Standard

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:25.510
instances.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:26.750
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And
it makes a lot of sense

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.624
that you're using App
Engine Standard because

00:13:28.624 --> 00:13:33.690
of the big spikiness that
you have in your traffic.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:37.310
That's a textbook use for--
use case for App Engine when

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:40.010
you have very unpredictable
workloads, very

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.070
spiky workloads, App Engine can
scale as high as it needs to.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.055
It can scale down as low as
it needs to all the way to 0,

00:13:46.055 --> 00:13:47.680
although obviously
with cameras sources

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.899
you probably never quite at 0.

00:13:49.899 --> 00:13:51.440
But it certainly
makes a lot of sense

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:52.880
that you're using
App Engine that way.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:53.810
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:58.130
For the people that are opening
the demo page, Camio.com/demo,

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:02.270
your queries are spinning
up instances in response

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:05.540
to your individual queries
because there's a mismatch out

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:09.050
of the workload for recording
and analyzing video versus

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:11.690
querying video because
you do much less querying.

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:13.850
So there's also the
dynamic provisioning

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:17.270
based on you guys going
to the website right now

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:18.470
and doing your own searches.

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:20.900
Those are spinning up
instances to make sure you

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.655
get good response times.

00:14:23.655 --> 00:14:24.780
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:27.210
So that helps us understand
what you're doing with a video

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:28.809
as it comes from the platform.

00:14:28.809 --> 00:14:31.350
Now let's talk about all those
users out there that are again

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.810
going to Camio.com/demo and
actually trying this out

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:34.800
for themselves.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.230
How are you delivering
that experience?

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:39.930
CARTER MASLAN: So there's
a front-end query parser

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.530
that determines based on what's
observed in your camera feeds

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.680
what you're asking for.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:51.120
So when you do a search like
Toyota Prius yesterday 1:00 PM,

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:53.730
it's parsing that, and it
knows that Toyota Prius

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:55.860
is make and model
of a car that you're

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.370
looking for in your feed.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.730
So the first step is just
understanding your query,

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:03.030
and that language has some
natural language processing

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:03.960
of the query itself.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:09.510
So you can say you know one
day ago at noon and also terms

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:12.590
and labels that are
applicable to your cameras.

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:14.760
And that's all served
from data store.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:21.720
So data store is indexed based
on both the time, the camera

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.210
source, the user, all that
stuff, to make it fast

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:25.230
and searchable.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:31.230
And the video itself is served
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:35.530
And in the process
of taking in video,

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:37.030
there's a whole
bunch of workflows

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:40.170
that are triggered from both
cloud data flow and just task

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:42.400
cues and Pub/Sub that
are triggered based

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.452
on the content of the video.

00:15:44.452 --> 00:15:45.910
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
So that's actually

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:46.870
I think an interesting point.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:48.700
Can you tell us a
little bit about how

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:53.480
you're using App Engine
task queues and data flow,

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:57.520
and what of the two things that
you're achieving with those?

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:00.681
CARTER MASLAN: I guess on the
task queue front, the big value

00:16:00.681 --> 00:16:02.680
that we actually didn't
anticipate when we first

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:06.280
started using task queue is
was just how resilient it is,

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.080
and we learned some--

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:10.460
in terms of if you have
a temporary Memcache

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:13.510
failure or a temporary
data store failure,

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:14.890
they're seamless to the users.

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:16.960
There's no impact
because it gets retried,

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:20.980
and it's a really
nice mechanism to have

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.930
communication among modules
and versions of your app.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:28.570
So in our case, our application
is split with a lot in Python

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:30.580
and a lot of the
front end in Java.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.490
We have modules that have
different kinds of workloads,

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:38.110
and task queues are easy way to
coordinate functions among them

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:44.890
reliably without any risk
of loss of data or loss

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:46.540
of the request.

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:48.070
The same thing with Pub/Sub.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:53.710
We use Pub/Sub for simple things
like if a batch operation is

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:56.740
completed, somebody might have
created a time lapse let's say.

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:59.440
So they may have said give
me a time lapse of my camera

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:04.920
from 8 AM Monday to 5 PM
Friday on a construction site.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.880
When that's completed,
we'll use Pub/Sub just

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:08.940
to notify people
of the completion,

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:10.589
including our internal
processes that

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:12.640
will do the billing and stuff.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:16.290
So it's just been a really
good, flexible mechanism

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:19.050
to coordinate
communication across all

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:21.009
the different modules
and application versions.

00:17:21.009 --> 00:17:22.841
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So
you're using task queues

00:17:22.841 --> 00:17:25.270
to do some of that
orchestration between modules.

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.599
You're using Pub/Sub to do
some of that communication

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:31.590
and things like notifications
or sent using Pub/Sub.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:33.945
What's the role of data
flow in this architecture?

00:17:33.945 --> 00:17:36.330
CARTER MASLAN: We're mainly
using the mappers for data

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:38.910
flow, not so much
the reducing, but we

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:42.540
do do periodic
summaries of counters

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:46.350
because Camio is all based
on usage based billing.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:51.360
So data flow helps us go over
massive amounts of events.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.870
Each camera-- each
day there's millions

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:56.340
of events to be summarized.

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:58.830
How many megabytes
have been uploaded?

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.570
How many important events?

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:01.770
How many unimportant events?

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:05.310
All of those things are
done via data flow including

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:08.580
the triggers to manage billing
cycles and things that are

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:10.120
driven off of those counters.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:11.370
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:13.830
So now we've covered what
you're doing with the video

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:16.310
as it comes in from the
camera through the box

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:17.580
and is ingested.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.130
We've covered what you're
doing to provide that user

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:21.609
experience in the UI.

00:18:21.609 --> 00:18:23.400
Tell me how you're
combining those two data

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:26.040
sources to actually do machine
learning in the platform?

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:26.873
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah.

00:18:26.873 --> 00:18:29.850
That's the key thing is
that the neural nets that

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:34.410
are being trained are on
a camera specific basis.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:37.590
So on the box itself,
there is something

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:40.800
that we call internally
adaptive motion filtering, which

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:45.120
is a set of neural nets
that are all concurrently

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:46.650
competing to be
right about what's

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:48.930
interesting for that
particular camera.

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:52.560
And the tricky thing in
cameras is that it's not just

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:55.924
about eliminating spurious
pixel motion because there

00:18:55.924 --> 00:18:57.840
are a lot of times where
a scene has something

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:01.500
that is a real object moving,
but it's just not interesting.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:07.230
You could have in the front of
a commercial building entrance

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:09.900
a tree that has just
a blowing branch that

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.180
is just swaying periodically.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.550
It is a real object moving,
but it's not interesting.

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:17.040
And so the box itself
has neural nets

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.950
that are learning that
that swing branch is not

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:20.940
interesting.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:25.680
But then the feedback
loops that come

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:27.170
from you using the products.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:30.540
So when you play an event or
you share an event or you save

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:33.270
an event or you give it
an explicit thumbs up

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.940
or thumbs down like
you're rating songs,

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:39.810
those all are now
collected training feedback

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:41.260
for your individual camera.

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:45.930
So even though
there's no visibility

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:48.060
into what your camera's
actually observing,

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.640
it still can train
itself based on the way

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:52.670
that you use the product.

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:57.840
And so those things surface in
the clean summary of your video

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:00.580
and the precision of
the search results.

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:02.640
But also in some
efficiency things.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.390
So I was going to show one
example of that efficiency

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:07.775
part of the machine learning.

00:20:07.775 --> 00:20:09.316
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, let's do that.

00:20:09.316 --> 00:20:12.175
So we'll do one more demo.

00:20:12.175 --> 00:20:16.800
CARTER MASLAN: This is a demo
that we call the matrix demo.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:19.530
And part of the
machine learning is

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:24.420
identifying what is interesting
versus not interesting.

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.040
In this case, there's
a lot of passing

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:30.840
street traffic that will be
in low res, fast time lapse.

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:33.570
And then when the person that's
approaching the sidewalk, which

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:37.230
is the nighttime library that
you want to actually observe,

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.180
it'll slow down into
full HD recording.

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.220
So this is why we call
it the matrix effect

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.220
because you'll see the
passing street traffic is

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:45.630
low res, fast time lapse.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:47.760
And then it slows
down to HD right

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:49.230
as the person comes into view.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:52.380
So we love that as an
example of machine learning

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:55.487
that is smart enough to
differentiate between passing

00:20:55.487 --> 00:20:57.570
street traffic and the
people that you're actually

00:20:57.570 --> 00:20:59.055
trying to protect at
night at the library.

00:20:59.055 --> 00:21:00.830
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah and
that's actually really amazing.

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:02.330
Not only does it
know that something

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:05.010
is happening that's different
than what was happening before,

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:06.750
it's something that
might be interesting.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.540
I now need to provide
additional context around that

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:13.330
and, like you said, give me
that full frame, high definition

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:14.310
feed now.

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:16.890
So someone who is
looking at this video

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:19.500
after the fact can
actually make decisions

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:20.616
around what did they see?

00:21:20.616 --> 00:21:22.740
Is it something that they
need to take action with?

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:22.950
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah.

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.140
And just from an
economics perspective,

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:28.770
you're saving both bandwidth
and storage and processing costs

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:30.510
by--

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:32.640
this was when we
first started talking,

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:35.160
we were talking about
the power efficiency

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.740
of having machine
learning make decisions

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:40.980
on what compute resources are
applied to any particular video

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:41.550
event.

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.860
And it's not dissimilar
from some of the things

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:45.960
that I've read on the
machine learning that

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.600
did a better job managing
power in Google data centers

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:49.330
than humans.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:51.150
And so it's similar
for your video

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:54.030
feeds where we're actually
having the machines help

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:57.300
you save bandwidth and
compute costs by knowing

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.655
what warrants the extra work.

00:21:59.655 --> 00:22:01.200
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:07.710
So now that we've seen the demo,
let's get back to our content

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:08.310
here.

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:12.160
And then next
question I have is now

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:17.760
that we understand what you're
doing with the data in GCP

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.730
and we understand some of
the use cases that are there,

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:24.552
quote unquote out of the box,
although not the Camio box,

00:22:24.552 --> 00:22:26.760
I imagine you have lots of
customers that come to you

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:29.100
and ask you to do things
that the platform has not

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.960
been built to do.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:35.040
How do you support new use
cases, new classifiers,

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:36.120
new requirements?

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.430
CARTER MASLAN: That was one
of the key design criteria

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:41.940
for a cameo is that we wanted
it to be a video processing

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.280
pipeline that's
open on both ends.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:46.230
So not only is it
open to all cameras,

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:48.840
but it's open to all
machine learning classifiers

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.460
and third party hooks.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:54.510
So hooks are the way that Camio
has an extensible pipeline,

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.210
and it's been used
for things as funny

00:22:57.210 --> 00:23:00.050
as like we had no
classifier for fish faces.

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:02.550
And there was a
cargo competition

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:05.100
to classify yellowfin
tuna versus bluefin

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:07.420
tuna versus marlin and sharks.

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:11.220
And it's as simple as defining
a hook in the Camio pipeline

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:14.100
where you can say when
the fish store opens

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:16.050
or when there's a
human on deck, I

00:23:16.050 --> 00:23:18.630
want you to run additional
classifiers that

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.430
are smart enough
to differentiate

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:22.290
between types of fish.

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:25.380
And that's not an expertise
we have, but in the old adage

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:27.660
there's more smart people
outside of your company

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:29.280
than in your company.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.680
That's the reason
that we have hooks

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:35.760
is that now all of that grunge
work of getting millions

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:39.380
of cameras connected and
being able to be processed,

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:41.520
they're now accessible
by third parties that

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:45.186
may specialize in
face recognition

00:23:45.186 --> 00:23:46.560
whether their fish
is [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:51.270
or other or access
control systems that

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:52.320
want to be integrated.

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:54.480
All of this is an
extensible thing.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:57.720
So you just register hook and
do the additional analysis

00:23:57.720 --> 00:23:58.756
that you'd like to do.

00:23:58.756 --> 00:23:59.880
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:01.326
So hooks are a
great way for folks

00:24:01.326 --> 00:24:02.700
to take advantage
of the pipeline

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:05.930
that you've built to do like
you said classification that

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:07.110
isn't there out of the box.

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:07.943
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah.

00:24:07.943 --> 00:24:11.490
In this example, this was not
even additional classification.

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:13.590
If you look at
this command, it's

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.710
basically just do a
quick walk through.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:18.900
You're saying when there's
a match on this query--

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:22.954
so it's much like if you've used
Google Alerts search alerts--

00:24:22.954 --> 00:24:24.120
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Absolutely.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:25.911
CARTER MASLAN: It's
the same thing in Camio

00:24:25.911 --> 00:24:29.190
when you pin a query that
now becomes an alert.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:32.220
That's the same thing that
drives the API for hooks.

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:36.150
You basically say whenever
there's people 2:00 AM to 5:00

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:37.590
AM on the rooftop--

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:39.570
this is an example of a query--

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:43.350
do this, which in this
case was a request

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:47.220
to have a call back URL that
would just schedule a guard--

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:48.810
on-premise guard tour.

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:52.710
So now instead of just having
your on-site security staff

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:56.370
roam, you're getting targeted
alerts based on query matches.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:59.910
So this cruel command, it's
a single simple command that

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:02.880
just says when that
query match happens,

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:07.590
call me back giving me the
payload of which camera source

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:10.780
observed it, what the
earliest and latest time was,

00:25:10.780 --> 00:25:13.200
and what we labeled it with.

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:15.660
And now that can drive
your scheduling system

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:16.660
for a guard tour.

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:19.170
It can drive your
notification systems.

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:21.210
It can do anything.

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.715
So this is the flexibility
in extending via hooks.

00:25:23.715 --> 00:25:24.840
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:27.930
So hooks are a great way to get
additional information that may

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:30.360
not be there out of the box.

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:32.107
How would you go
about understanding

00:25:32.107 --> 00:25:33.190
when things are happening.

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:35.820
So example I know
that I saw people

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:38.520
in the rooftop between
the specific hours,

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:41.730
but what if I want to
know exactly when someone

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.130
opened that hatch for example
and started climbing up?

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:45.395
How do I go about doing that?

00:25:45.395 --> 00:25:47.860
CARTER MASLAN: So there's--

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:50.400
maybe we could look at
annotations quickly.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:53.160
This is an example where
part of the pipeline

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.640
enables you to correlate
any label to a time stamp.

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:58.770
And that means that any
device, whether it's

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:02.070
a door sensor or an
access control bajan,

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:05.010
can now be a source of
annotating a video feed.

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:08.430
So if you want to say
show me every time

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:10.890
that the fish door
was open, it's

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:14.310
simple to see that in video
because this one command just

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:19.320
says put these labels, badge 1,
2, 3 unlocked, as a door unlock

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:19.990
event.

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:23.790
Now whenever you search for show
me the times that this door was

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:27.180
unlocked, you'll see the video
that matches those events that

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:28.500
come from those sensors.

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:30.330
So it's not just about
the video content,

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:33.000
but it's also third-party
devices and sensors

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.190
that can be integrated
into that video content.

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:36.960
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:39.150
So now we've covered hooks
and annotations, which

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:41.500
are which-- ways by which
other people can, again,

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:45.120
take advantage of the platform
and the pipeline you've built.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:46.500
Thank you so much for that.

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:48.990
Before we get into
the Q&amp;A, I wanted

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:51.930
to ask you one last question.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:53.730
As you've built out
your infrastructure

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:56.682
and your platform on GCP,
are there any lessons

00:26:56.682 --> 00:26:58.890
that you've learned that
you think our audience would

00:26:58.890 --> 00:26:59.850
benefit from?

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:01.020
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah.

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:04.650
I think if I were to summarize
some of the top lessons

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:09.840
from the past years of working
with GCP, one of the first ones

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:13.010
is in terms of your data,
if you're using data store,

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:16.159
make sure you think in
terms of key value buckets.

00:27:16.159 --> 00:27:18.200
Because if you come from
a relational background,

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:21.500
you may be tempted to just
take your relational thinking

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:23.030
into data store.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.520
And if you want
to avoid problems

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:28.730
with eventual consistency
and indexing update times

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:30.890
and things like that,
think of your data

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:34.670
as atomic key value
buckets rather than

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:36.370
as rows in a relational table.

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:37.640
That's probably one.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:39.056
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah makes sense.

00:27:39.056 --> 00:27:41.600
CARTER MASLAN: Two, in
terms of performance,

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:45.080
we found that almost always when
you profile your application

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:48.590
for performance, RPCs,
remote procedure calls,

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:49.310
are the culprit.

00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:55.550
So think about how you
do things in parallel.

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:58.091
So if you have a list
of events that you're

00:27:58.091 --> 00:27:59.840
going to display on
the screen like you're

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:02.450
seeing with our search
results, do a batch

00:28:02.450 --> 00:28:05.750
GET so that you've
got a list of 60 keys

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:08.390
that you're fetching in
one RPC that it can all

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:11.030
be in parallel rather than
doing that sequentially.

00:28:11.030 --> 00:28:15.410
So that's another lesson.

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:20.230
I'm trying to think of other
highlights that we've learned.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:21.790
But those are two
starting points

00:28:21.790 --> 00:28:22.810
YURI GRINSHTEYN: No,
those are very good.

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:24.268
And on that second
point, I'll make

00:28:24.268 --> 00:28:26.799
a bit of a product
pitch, which is folks

00:28:26.799 --> 00:28:28.340
if you're interested
in understanding

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:30.950
the performance of the
RPCs in your application,

00:28:30.950 --> 00:28:32.827
take a look at the
trace functionality

00:28:32.827 --> 00:28:33.660
within stack driver.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:36.245
It can really expose
those slow-running RPCs

00:28:36.245 --> 00:28:38.120
until you get exactly
where some of the links

00:28:38.120 --> 00:28:40.480
in your application
calls may be.

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:41.150
Well, awesome.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:42.560
Thank you so much.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.410
I think now we will
take a quick break.

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:47.780
Please stay tuned
for the live Q&amp;A,

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:49.848
and we'll be back in
less than one minute.

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:35.680
Welcome back.

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:38.179
Let's go through some of the
questions that you have for us.

00:30:38.179 --> 00:30:40.780
Our first question
is, how does Camio

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:43.540
charge its customers given
how much heavy processing

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:44.916
you're doing?

00:30:44.916 --> 00:30:46.540
CARTER MASLAN: That's
a great question.

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:51.100
We at one point had started
with a fixed monthly price,

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:55.000
and we realized quickly the
workloads vary so much by what

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.090
the camera is observing.

00:30:57.090 --> 00:30:58.930
So you can have a really
inexpensive camera

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:01.930
in a quiet server room with
credit card processing data

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:06.560
and a really busy camera
observing a parking garage.

00:31:06.560 --> 00:31:08.750
And so we have
usage-based pricing.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:14.110
So you can pay on a
per gigabyte basis,

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:16.460
and that enables you to
tune the machine learning.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:18.940
We also wanted to have that
incentive where as it gets

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:21.520
smarter, your prices go down.

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:24.520
And so basically
Camio has custom plans

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:28.390
that our partners
offer that are all

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:30.820
tuned to your specific
video processing

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:33.595
needs including
duration of storage.

00:31:33.595 --> 00:31:37.090
Do you need 90-day
storage, a year storage?

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:39.100
What kinds of advanced
analytics do you want?

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:44.410
Do you want card classification
or just OCR text recognition?

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:48.940
What amount of events do you
want processed every day?

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:50.440
All of these things
are variables

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:52.780
that go into the
pricing of a plan

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:55.090
so that you get the
lowest possible price

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:56.105
for your workload.

00:31:56.105 --> 00:31:57.605
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Great, so it sounds

00:31:57.605 --> 00:31:59.100
like the two real
metrics are going

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:02.000
to be how much video
you're processing,

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.459
and then the second part is
how complex the processing is.

00:32:04.459 --> 00:32:05.250
CARTER MASLAN: Yep.

00:32:05.250 --> 00:32:05.740
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK.

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:06.430
Great.

00:32:06.430 --> 00:32:07.180
All right.

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:10.000
So next question is if I
see something interesting

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.580
happen in the feed, how
do I go about sharing

00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:14.500
the video to the world.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:17.950
And is this the same as
traditional video sharing,

00:32:17.950 --> 00:32:20.205
or are you doing
something different there?

00:32:20.205 --> 00:32:23.380
CARTER MASLAN: It's different
in the sense that our share via

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:25.360
links-- we call
them query links--

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:29.370
when you share a link, it's
like sharing a constrained view

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:33.070
onto a feed that you
can revoke at any time.

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:36.490
So for everybody that's
used Google Docs sharing,

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:39.040
you have an option where
you can either add somebody

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:43.210
as a permanent collaborator,
or you can just send a link.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:44.800
And so we have that same model.

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:47.080
You can either send
a link with-- so

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:50.470
that when you're doing these
demos on the Camio.com demo

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:52.360
page, that's an
example of query links

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:55.840
because they give you the right
to access the content just

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:59.050
by having the token
that is in that query.

00:32:59.050 --> 00:33:01.300
But if you have somebody
that is part of your security

00:33:01.300 --> 00:33:02.920
operations staff,
for example, they

00:33:02.920 --> 00:33:04.960
may be a permanent
guest on the account,

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:07.330
much like a collaborator
on a document.

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:09.940
But the difference
in Camio's case

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:14.530
is that the sharing
can be constrained

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:16.250
to specific segments.

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:19.060
So it's like if you could
share a Google doc but only

00:33:19.060 --> 00:33:21.630
one paragraph of the document.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:23.590
And I'll give an example
of that if I could do

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:27.310
a demo from a city camera here.

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:30.430
Let me just-- this is an
example of a city camera

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:33.680
that is the last demo
link on the demo page.

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:39.700
And this is only 9:45 to
10:00 AM on last Friday.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:42.640
And so the interesting thing
is if I try to scroll earlier,

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:47.800
it stops at 9:45 even though
there's a lot of other video.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:49.990
So this is the example
of sharing a paragraph

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:51.070
within a doc.

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:54.100
But the interesting
thing is I can still

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:59.090
search within that query
link that's shared with me.

00:33:59.090 --> 00:34:01.360
So if I want to do
this search, I'll

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:05.095
just say what I was really
looking for is a white Toyota.

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:10.300
Now let me just see
if there was-- there

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:11.820
was no white Toyota
at that point.

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:13.746
So let me just certainly do--

00:34:13.746 --> 00:34:17.730
I'll just look for a white car.

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:20.690
And now I'm subsetting
my search to just

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:23.989
be the events that had
a white car in them.

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:27.889
And so you can do the same
thing with a constrained period

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:34.159
of time where I can say I
was just looking for 9:50 AM

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:39.590
to 9:52 AM on Friday.

00:34:39.590 --> 00:34:42.500
And now because that is
within the constraints

00:34:42.500 --> 00:34:46.580
of the 9:45 to 10:00
sharing, I can still do this.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:50.239
So if you take a scenario like
the Boston Marathon bombing

00:34:50.239 --> 00:34:54.380
where they had 1,000 people
review video for three days

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:59.060
to find the suspects, this
kind of crowdsourced review

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:02.720
gives you a secure way to
share a very limited portion

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:05.630
of your video stream
but enables people

00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:08.870
to do certain refinements
and additional queries

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:11.000
within the constraints
of what you've shared.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:12.510
And you can add--

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:15.850
you can share by link, but you
can also add people to the link

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:17.730
so that they still
have to authenticate.

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:21.200
So there's-- that's the other
level of sharing is even when

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:24.860
you share via link, you can
still additionally require that

00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:26.440
somebody be authenticated.

00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:27.300
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So
it sounds like you

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:29.250
have some very powerful
sharing capabilities

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:32.840
both in allowing other folks
to view and collaborate

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:36.020
in the streams but also
control what they can actually

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:38.900
see to control their access
to perhaps specific slices

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:39.410
of time--

00:35:39.410 --> 00:35:40.810
CARTER MASLAN: Yeah or zones.

00:35:40.810 --> 00:35:42.440
Like you could say
even if they only

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:44.480
see people that are
coming out of an elevator,

00:35:44.480 --> 00:35:47.660
you could have a zone
elevator share only that zone

00:35:47.660 --> 00:35:50.210
and then they can't see
what's happening over

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:51.740
in another office room.

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:53.839
It would only be
the events that are

00:35:53.839 --> 00:35:55.130
intersecting with the elevator.

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:56.255
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Fantastic.

00:35:56.255 --> 00:35:58.046
I think that also
answers our next question

00:35:58.046 --> 00:35:59.640
about that
granularity of access.

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:01.905
I think you've done a
great job of covering that.

00:36:01.905 --> 00:36:05.650
So our final question is
actually around the box

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:06.770
believe it or not.

00:36:06.770 --> 00:36:09.680
So you're using your gateway
on a site to do pre-processing

00:36:09.680 --> 00:36:11.820
and then sending
it to the cloud.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:15.980
How many parallel streams can
this gateway actually manage?

00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:18.530
Or maybe a broader
question is how do you

00:36:18.530 --> 00:36:20.990
go about deploying a
system by which there

00:36:20.990 --> 00:36:23.225
are many, many, many cameras?

00:36:23.225 --> 00:36:25.190
CARTER MASLAN: So
the simple answer

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:27.050
is I could just give
you the rough metrics.

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.240
It's because it's dependent
on the streams that

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:30.090
are being monitored.

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:36.750
So if you have 4K streams at 30
frames per second versus 720p

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:39.304
streams at 20 frames per
second, that's a big difference.

00:36:39.304 --> 00:36:40.220
YURI GRINSHTEYN: True.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.950
CARTER MASLAN: But
the general point

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:46.610
is that it scales based
on the number of streams

00:36:46.610 --> 00:36:48.200
and the characteristics
of the stream.

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:51.590
So to give an example,
just if you just

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:55.910
have three HD 1080p streams
at 30 frames per second,

00:36:55.910 --> 00:37:00.390
you can use a tiny little box
that will handle those three.

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:02.180
And then if you go
to six, you can just

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:05.060
linearly stack more boxes.

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:07.520
But you can also run
boxes a virtual machine

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:09.700
on any set of servers.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:14.450
So if you have 120
cameras on campus,

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:16.934
you can run that on a single
server if it's beefy enough.

00:37:16.934 --> 00:37:18.100
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Understood.

00:37:18.100 --> 00:37:21.960
CARTER MASLAN: So we have
tools that estimate--

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:24.300
you enter in the
characteristics of your stream

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:29.870
like I have 1080p streams at 30
frames per second at this four

00:37:29.870 --> 00:37:31.770
megabit encoding rate.

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:34.250
And then it spits out
your recommended number

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:39.460
